#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199370} :activity/id #uuid "9b6bcbf9-9e97-506c-ac92-2753f429a355" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050851 :topic/id #uuid "6bf185a6-38f8-583f-b073-169dac3d7fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4208} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050148}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403440}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "82c5626f-5ffa-5852-9567-16e71d3f88de" :step/math-academy-id 19456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403717}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"} :step/id #uuid "fd96ee0e-953b-5e30-a73d-de454e088881" :step/math-academy-id 20423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199499 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d437807f6e580bc6c2c16cacca1ea7c8e49ed4653717cdb3c9e27.png)\n\nNote that the intersection of the paraboloid and plane is given by the solution to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z=x+y-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/52/520b57cf872ede31d4397d59a833c565147d82a45be1d6b2634f82e1effdff8c.png)\n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0d1b4d-a008-573c-b368-9ab2bfc7f41c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7833 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Surfaces With Planes"} :step/id #uuid "d692d6c4-d307-5093-8885-932a55dd02f4" :step/math-academy-id 45271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=-t \\\\ z=-t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "90b0a614-08e3-5d8b-bcf4-28aacc99e1ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "152c742b-df15-5eb5-a164-c09238c5b2e6" :step/math-academy-id 45272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199234}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403454}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "62b86b82-79e2-51cc-abf3-68181195c0c8" :step/math-academy-id 45293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199369}}] :activity/title "Intersections of Lines and Planes With Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198505}]}
